      "message": "This type of behavior you want is incredibly difficult to get right for the user. And, the code as you have it doesn\u0027t have the tools to solve any of the nuance that comes with the problem.\n\nFor instance, how do you tell between a remote device that _wanted_ to disconnect and one that accidentally went out of range? Imagine constantly reconnecting a users device when that\u0027s not what they want? That\u0027s infinitely worse than the user going to settings to reconnect.\n\nWe would need a much larger effort of gathering context in the native layer around why something happened, so we could pass it up to the platform. And even then, I might prefer to defer reconnection decisions to logic outside the stack, instead giving the context through something like disconnect_reason (i.e. a \"page timeout\" vs. a remote user disconnected, etc.).\n\nPlus, if all you want is out of range events, then some of this context might already be in the framework at the ACL level. You might be able to have an external to the stack policy that just looks for ACL disconnect events with the right reasons and reconnects everything. That way you can get it correct for your products without us in the stack making assumptions about how everyone wants to run their policy logic.",
